+# Coupled-run status
+
+`spiral-coder coupled-status --run-dir PATH` reads a native coupled-run record and prints one JSON summary. It is read-only: it makes no provider calls, runs no tools, writes no files, and needs no API key. `PATH` may be relative; it is resolved from the caller's current working directory.
+
+The installed command forwards the same arguments:
+
+```text
+spiral coupled-status --run-dir PATH
+```
+
+`wire_requests` counts every durable entry in a ledger's `requests` array, including failed and in-flight attempts. `task_requests` counts entries whose `charge` is `task`; an omitted charge uses the native task default. Text logs and report prose are never used to infer usage.
+
+An interrupted iteration may have an absent ledger. In that case pending task and wire usage, in-flight requests, and aggregate used/remaining totals are `null`, not zero. Limits remain known. A present ledger must be valid and is counted once.
+
+The `completed` status records what the native manifest says: it is not an independent verification, qualification, or promotion authorization. A stopped record has a native stop reason; otherwise the recorded status is running.
+
+The following is the completed synthetic example produced by `scripts.status_fixture.make` and `scripts.status_fixture.expected('completed')`:
+
+```json
+{"version": 1, "status": "completed", "stop_reason": "completed", "iterations": [{"iteration": 1, "task_id": "t1", "termination": "completed", "task_requests": 2, "wire_requests": 3, "changed_files": ["src/a.rs"], "newly_verified": ["cargo test -q"]}], "pending": null, "requests": {"task_used": 2, "wire_used": 3, "task_limit": 192, "wire_limit": 216, "task_remaining": 190, "wire_remaining": 213}}
+```
